Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: Third Harmonic Above the Service Road
- Story & Context: The debut begins at 110 BPM with a four-bar broken-beat loop and a single low alphorn fundamental. Lukas then jumps to the third harmonic, leaving a wide open interval that Nia refuses to resolve with bass. Instead, Selma answers one eighth-note late with a dry suspended Rhodes dyad while Elias crawls upward chromatically beneath the held brass pitch. Every four bars, Nia changes the kick and snare emphasis without changing Lukas’s two-note call. By the fifth cycle, the identical alphorn phrase feels as if it is standing on a different meter even though tempo and bar length remain constant. The middle section removes kick entirely and lets frame drum, clarinet key noise, and Rhodes tine knocks support one exposed upper partial. The final hit is not a cadence: Lukas returns to the original fundamental while the drums continue for two bars after him.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Snow-covered mountain service road crossing beneath a projected contour map, one dark alphorn extending diagonally through the frame, orange rescue marker at the roadside, prepared Rhodes keys and contrabass-clarinet bell in shallow focus. No full lyrics; optional spoken fragments limited to “third harmonic,” “same call,” “new footing,” and “keep climbing sideways.”

Track 2: Contour Line Refuses the Downbeat
- Story & Context: Nia programs the EP’s most unstable groove at 116 BPM, with the strongest kick repeatedly arriving after beat one. Lukas holds a higher alphorn partial across the displaced accents while Selma uses clipped quartal chords to mark only phrase endings. Elias plays a three-note contrabass-clarinet figure that begins on the expected downbeat, creating a deliberate disagreement between bass motion and drum emphasis. Halfway through, the entire programmed kit mutes for four bars while hand percussion maintains the pulse. Lukas repeats the same call, proving that the rhythmic displacement has changed perception rather than the written brass line. When the beat returns, it does so with a new kick map and no crash or riser.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Large faded avalanche contour chart pinned to a concrete wall, one elevation line continuing straight while black drum-grid marks underneath shift left and right, alphorn mouthpiece and muted cowbell resting on a steel desk. Spoken fragments limited to “line stays,” “downbeat moves,” “do not redraw it,” and “enter late.”

Track 3: The Valley Answers One Partial Lower
- Story & Context: The closer begins outside the station at dusk. Lukas plays one high natural harmonic toward the opposite valley wall while a distant microphone captures the short return. Nia samples nothing; the acoustic echo remains live. Elias answers the reflected pitch one partial lower with a breath-heavy low note, while Selma places a single prepared-Rhodes dyad between the outgoing horn and its return. Over several minutes the drum pattern becomes simpler, moving from detailed broken beat to kick, rim, and brush only. The final section removes electronics entirely. Lukas plays one last call, waits for the mountain return, and stops. Elias does not answer. The record ends on the small mechanical sound of Selma releasing the Rhodes sustain pedal inside the station.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Blue-hour alpine valley seen from a concrete communications bay, long alphorn aimed into open snow air, distant opposing slope under fading light, one small room microphone on a stand, prepared Rhodes visible inside behind frost-edged glass. Spoken fragments limited to “send one partial,” “wait for the wall,” “answer lower,” and “leave the last return alone.”

